// Demonstration of the `er` package — entity-relationship diagrams in Chen notation, a thin
// layer of conventions over the diagram layer.
//
// Render with:  texish scripts/er-demo.script

\use{er}

\def headline {\italic{The er package} \hfil page \the\pageno}
\def footline {\hfil — \the\pageno — \hfil}
\set raggedbottom {1}
\def centerline c {\hbox to:\hsize {\hfil\c\hfil}}

\font lmroman 12 regular

\bold{Entity-relationship diagrams}

In Chen notation an entity is a rectangle, a relationship a diamond, and an attribute an
ellipse, joined by undirected lines that carry cardinalities. A key attribute is underlined,
a weak entity and an identifying relationship double their outline, and total participation
doubles the connecting line. The schema below records employees, the departments they work
for, and their dependents.

\vskip 12pt

\centerline{\picture width:6in height:4.3in {
  // attributes across the top
  \keyattribute{ssn}{at 0.55in 3.8in}{ssn}
  \attribute{ename}{at 1.7in 3.9in}{name}
  \attribute{dname}{at 4.3in 3.9in}{name}
  \keyattribute{dnum}{at 5.45in 3.8in}{number}

  // the entity — relationship — entity spine
  \entity{emp}{at 1.3in 2.8in}{Employee}
  \relationship{wf}{at 3in 2.8in}{Works For}
  \entity{dept}{at 4.7in 2.8in}{Department}

  // a weak entity, identified by the employee it depends on
  \weakrelationship{has}{at 1.3in 1.7in}{Has}
  \weakentity{dep}{at 1.3in 0.7in}{Dependent}
  \keyattribute{depname}{at 3in 1in}{name}

  // attribute links
  \connect{emp}{ssn}
  \connect{emp}{ename}
  \connect{dept}{dname}
  \connect{dept}{dnum}
  \connect{dep}{depname}

  // relationship links, with cardinalities
  \connect[N]{emp}{wf}
  \connect[1]{wf}{dept}
  \connect[1]{emp}{has}
  \connecttotal[N]{has}{dep}
}}
